-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Current-State and Unresolved-Work Representation
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+Retrieving a visit (or listing visits filtered by status) returns for each visit:
+- `visit_id` and `status` (`current` or `needs_review`).
+- `current_revision`: populated with the sole active revision when `status` is `current`; `null` when `status` is `needs_review` so callers cannot mistake an unresolved visit for a settled exportable record.
+- `active_heads`: the list of unresolved head revisions (one revision when `current`, two or more competing revisions when `needs_review`), each with its `revision_id`, `parent_revision_ids`, `author_id`, `received_at`, `resolution_metadata` (if created by coordinator resolution), and `payload`.
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+Revision-history retrieval returns the complete DAG of revisions for a visit ordered by `received_at` (with `revision_id` as a deterministic tie-breaker) so coordinators and audit tools can trace how every exported value evolved from original worker submissions.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+## Coordinator Resolution Operation
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+To resolve a visit in `needs_review`, an authenticated coordinator submits a resolution request containing:
+- `visit_id`
+- `resolved_revision_ids`: the exact set of competing active head `revision_id`s the coordinator reviewed and is superseding.
+- `payload`: the reconciled complete visit payload (either matching one of the competing revisions or combining values after coordinator inspection).
+- `resolution_note` (optional): coordinator explanation of the reconciliation.
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+If `resolved_revision_ids` matches the visit's current `active_heads` set, the store creates a new immutable revision whose parent list is `resolved_revision_ids`, records the coordinator's `author_id` and server timestamp, replaces those active heads with the new revision as the sole active head, and transitions the visit to `status: current`.
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+If a field worker submitted another revision while the coordinator was reviewing (so `resolved_revision_ids` no longer covers all `active_heads`, or names a non-head revision), the resolution is rejected as stale so the newly arrived submission is not silently superseded.
+
+## Downstream Contract Obligations
+
+- **tablet-sync**: Retains the `revision_id` of the last synced visit state as `base_revision_id` for local edits, sends full visit payloads with an `idempotency_key`, and surfaces `needs_review` status to workers when a submission branches rather than fast-forwarding.
+- **review-export**: Queries visits with `status: needs_review`, presents `active_heads` and their common ancestor from revision history for side-by-side comparison, submits coordinator resolutions against `resolved_revision_ids`, and includes only visits with `status: current` (`current_revision`) in the Friday partner CSV.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrent visit edit policy | Whole-visit revisions with explicit `base_revision_id` and coordinator resolution of competing heads ([decision doc](decisions/concurrent-visit-edits.md)) | Field-aware three-way merge with base snapshots and per-field merge rules; last-write-wins by server receipt time | Scientific fields within a visit (such as species identity and cover percentage) can be coupled in ways field names do not express. Whole-visit revisions avoid silently combining scientifically incompatible edits and keep the system operable by two coordinators without maintaining custom field-merge rules. Last-write-wins violates the tenet against silently discarding submitted edits. |
+| Visit state while competing heads exist | `current_revision` is `null` and `status` is `needs_review` until a coordinator resolves the heads | Keep the earliest-arriving head as `current_revision` while flagging review work | Arrival order reflects tablet connectivity rather than scientific authority, and leaving `current_revision` unset prevents downstream export callers from exporting an unreviewed competing state. |
+| Resolution concurrency guard | Require `resolved_revision_ids` to match the visit's current `active_heads` set | Resolve only the named subset of heads; unconditionally overwrite all active heads | Requiring an exact match prevents a coordinator from accidentally overwriting a field submission that arrived while the comparison screen was open, preserving the no-silent-discard tenet. |
